  },
  "SC2051": {
    "code": "SC2051",
    "title": "Bash doesn't support variables in brace range expansions.",
    "fix": "Bash doesn't support variables in brace range expansions.\r",
    "rationale": "In Bash, brace expansion happens before variable expansion. This means that brace expansion will not account for variables.\n\nFor integers, use an arithmetic for loop instead. For zero-padded numbers or letters, use of eval may be warranted:\n\n```bash\nfrom=\"a\" to=\"m\"\nfor c in $(eval \"echo {$from..$to}\"); do echo \"$c\"; done\n```\n\nor more carefully (if `from`/`to` could be user input, or if the brace expansion could have spaces):\n\n```bash\nfrom=\"a\" to=\"m\"\nwhile IFS= read -d '' -r c\ndo\n  echo \"Read $c\"\ndone <  <(eval \"printf '%s\\0' $(printf \"{%q..%q}.jpg\" \"$from\" \"$to\")\")\n```",
    "problematicCode": "for i in {1..$n}\ndo\n  echo \"$i\"\ndone",
    "correctCode": "for ((i=1; i <= n; i++))\ndo\n  echo \"$i\"\ndone"
  },
  "SC2053": {
    "code": "SC2053",
    "title": "Quote the rhs of `=` in `[[ ]]` to prevent glob matching.",
    "fix": "Quote the rhs of `=` in `[[ ]]` to prevent glob matching.",
    "rationale": "When the right-hand side of `=`, `==` or `!=` is unquoted in `[[ .. ]]`, it will be treated like a glob.\n\nThis has some unexpected consequences like `[[ $var = $var ]]` being false (for `var='[a]'`), or `[[ $foo = $bar ]]` giving a different result from `[[ $bar = $foo ]]`.\n\nThe most common intention is to compare one variable to another as strings, in which case the right-hand side must be quoted.",
    "problematicCode": "[[ $a = $b ]]",
    "correctCode": "[[ $a = \"$b\" ]]"
  },
  "SC2054": {
