  "SC2117": {
    "code": "SC2117",
    "title": "To run commands as another user, use `su -c` or `sudo`.",
    "fix": "To run commands as another user, use `su -c` or `sudo`.",
    "rationale": "It's commonly believed that `su` makes a session run as another user. In reality, it starts an entirely new shell, independent of the one currently running your script.\n\n`su; whoami` will start a root shell and wait for it to exit before running `whoami`. It will not start a root shell and then proceed to run `whoami` in it.\n\nTo run commands as another user, use `sudo some command` or `su -c 'some command'`. `sudo` is preferred when available, as it doesn't require additional quoting and can be configured to run passwordless if desired.",
