  },
  "SC2255": {
    "code": "SC2255",
    "title": "`[ ]` does not apply arithmetic evaluation. Evaluate with `$((..))` for numbers, or use string comparator for strings.",
    "fix": " `[ ]` does not apply arithmetic evaluation. Evaluate with `$((..))` for numbers, or use string comparator for strings.",
    "rationale": "When using `[[ .. ]]` with numerical comparators (`-eq`, `-lt`, etc), the value on either side will be evaluated as an arithmetic expression. This means that `2*3` will be evaluated to `6`, and `x` will be evaluated to the contents of the variable `$x`.\n\nWhen using `[ .. ]`, this does not happen. `2*3` and `x` will both be considered invalid numbers. Instead, use e.g. `$((2*3))` to evaluate the expression before passing it to `[ .. ]`.\n\nAlternatively, if the expression should be considered a string, quote the expression and use a string comparison operator like `=` and `!=`. ",
    "problematicCode": "[ 2*3 -eq array[i] ]",
    "correctCode": "[ $((2*3)) -eq $((array[i])) ]"
  },
  "SC2256": {
